Allocation logic

/{subscriber}/allocate-production-orders

Request Body:
{
    "fromDateTime": "2025-01-01T00:00:00Z", 
    // start date time to allocate production orders. Must be always starting on the next day from current date.
}

Get all ProductionOrders in "Ready To Produce" status and group by service type and priority

Retrieve all related work schedule related with the service types from ProductionOrders

Retrieve the all available work resources by service type starting from the "fromDateTime" parameter, verifying any existing allocations 
for the production orders.

Example:

Assuming that "Lathe" work schedule is 8 hours per day, from Monday to Friday and the setup time is 90 minutes
and earliest avaible date time for workResource "Lathe" is 2025-01-01T15:00:00Z, 2025-01-01 would only have 2 hours available,
it would be not a good approach to allocate the production order to "Lathe" work resource on 2025-01-01, as it would only
have 30 minutes available on 2025-01-01. So, the earliest avaible date time for "Lathe" would be:

workSchedule.startTime = 08:00:00Z
workSchedule.endTime = 17:00:00Z

if workSchedule.endTime - (ealiestAvailableDateTime + setupTimeInHours) >= 3 hours then
    allocationStartDateTime = (ealiestAvailableDateTime + setupTimeInHours)
else
    allocationStartDateTime = next day from ealiestAvailableDateTime


Calculate the amount of time available per day for each work resource, based on work resource's schedule. 
It should be done following the formula:

hourAvailablePerDay = workSchedule.shiftDurationInMinutes/60


Example:
fromDateTime = 2025-01-01T00:00:00Z
ealiestAvailableDateTime = 2025-01-01T15:00:00Z
hoursworkResource.setupTimeInMinutes = 90
setupTimeInHours = hoursworkResource.setupTimeInMinutes / 60
productionOrder.estimatedTime = 25 hours
workSchedule.shiftDurationInMinutes = 480 minutes
hourAvailablePerDay = 480/60 = 8 hours
workSchedule.startTime = 08:00:00Z
workSchedule.endTime = 17:00:00Z
totalHours = productionOrder.estimatedTime + setupTimeInHours (26.5 hour taking hourAvailablePerDay into account)
daysNeeded = (int) Math.ceil(totalHours / hoursPerDay);

will result in 4 days allocation for "Lathe" work resources, (starting from 2025-01-02T08:00:00Z as 2025-01-01 has only 2 hours available):

IMPORTANT: the allocation day interval must be validated if its a work data according to resource's work schedule. For example,
a work resource might be available also in weekends, so the allocation days can be in weekends also.

allocation1:
startDateTime = 2025-01-02T08:00:00Z
endDateTime = 2025-01-02T17:00:00Z
hoursPerDay = 8

allocation2:
startDateTime = 2025-01-03T08:00:00Z
endDateTime = 2025-01-03T17:00:00Z
hoursPerDay = 8

allocation3:
startDateTime = 2025-01-04T08:00:00Z
endDateTime = 2025-01-04T17:00:00Z
hoursPerDay = 8

allocation4:
startDateTime = 2025-01-05T08:00:00Z
endDateTime = 2025-01-05T10:30:00Z
hoursPerDay = 2.5

This allocations should be saved in the database as ProductionOrderWorkAllocation entity.